---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";

---
<Layout title="hobbies">
    <section class="bg-slate-100 dark:bg-muted dark:text-foreground">
        <div class="max-w-3xl space-y-4">
            <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-[0.3em] text-slate-500 dark:text-muted-foreground">Hobbies</p>
            <h1>What I spend time exploring outside of direct project work</h1>
            <p>
                I keep this page separate from About because these interests are part of how I build taste and
                perspective, even when they are not directly tied to client work.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bg-white dark:bg-background">
        <div class="grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-5 dark:border-border dark:bg-card">
                <h2>Technology rabbit holes</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">I like exploring tools and systems that stretch me a bit beyond my default web stack.</p>
                <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5">
                    <li>
                        <span class="font-semibold">Docker</span>: a continuing area of practice whenever I want cleaner local environments and more realistic deployment thinking.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="font-semibold">Sequelize</span>: useful partly because it sharpens my sense of what a backend abstraction helps with and what it can hide too aggressively.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <span class="font-semibold">Embedded tooling</span>: I have enjoyed learning more about PlatformIO, microcontrollers, and the mindset shift that comes with them.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-slate-50 p-5 dark:border-border dark:bg-card">
                <h2>Non-software interests</h2>
                <p class="mt-3">
                    Axe throwing has become one of the clearest examples of the kind of challenge I enjoy: technique,
                    repetition, feedback, and the slow process of getting something difficult to feel natural.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-3">
                    That same pattern tends to show up in how I learn software too, which is part of why I keep this
                    page around instead of flattening everything into a standard professional bio.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</Layout>
